{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"caption\":\"United States v. Zelaya-Veliz\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-02-16\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"94 F.4th 321; Nos. 22-4656, 22-4659, 22-4669, 22-4670, 22-4684\",\"proposition\":\"Applied Leon despite extraordinarily broad Facebook all-data warrants, including one without a temporal limit. The court declined to impose a categorical temporal-limit rule and reasoned that unsettled law governing social-media warrants made reliance objectively reasonable. Certiorari was denied November 18, 2024.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"good_faith/Leon/digital_warrants/temporal_limits\",\"discovery_path\":\"Good-faith and digital-warrant searches -> Fourth Circuit opinion -> Supreme Court docket confirming date, consolidated cases, and certiorari disposition: https://www.supremecourt.gov/search.aspx?filename=%2Fdocket%2Fdocketfiles%2Fhtml%2Fpublic%2F24-5092.html\",\"confidence\":0.98}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Pemberton\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-02-28\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"94 F.4th 1130\",\"proposition\":\"Applied the good-faith exception to a pre-McGirt Oklahoma state warrant issued for a search in Indian country, notwithstanding the issuing court's jurisdictional error. Officers reasonably relied on the prevailing legal landscape and the judge's authority; the record showed no deliberate, reckless, or grossly negligent disregard of Fourth Amendment rights.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"good_faith/Leon-Herring/jurisdictionally_invalid_warrant\",\"discovery_path\":\"Jurisdictionally void warrant and good-faith searches -> Pemberton citations and detailed treatment in United States v. Bailey: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca10/23-5044/23-5044-2024-06-24.html\",\"confidence\":0.93}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. O'Neill\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-02-29\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 22-3793\",\"proposition\":\"Applied Leon after upholding a finding that false peer-to-peer-file-sharing assertions copied into warrant affidavits were negligent rather than reckless. Even assuming probable cause was absent, the affidavits were not bare-bones or facially deficient enough to make reliance unreasonable; the dissent viewed the repeated falsehood as reckless.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"good_faith/Leon-Herring/Franks_affidavit_misstatements\",\"discovery_path\":\"Good-faith, Franks, and affidavit-misstatement searches -> published Sixth Circuit opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca6/22-3793/22-3793-2024-02-29.html\",\"confidence\":0.98}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Sanders (en banc)\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit en banc\",\"date\":\"2024-06-28\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 21-5945\",\"proposition\":\"Vacated the 2023 panel opinion and affirmed because the residential warrant had a sufficient probable-cause nexus, leaving the panel's Leon-only route nonprecedential. Separate en banc opinions sharply disputed when courts may bypass the constitutional question and begin with good faith.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"good_faith/Leon/adjudication_sequence/en_banc_limiting\",\"discovery_path\":\"En banc and good-faith sequencing searches -> published Sixth Circuit en banc opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca6/21-5945/21-5945-2024-06-28.html\",\"confidence\":0.98}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Green\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-07-02\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 19-4348\",\"proposition\":\"Held that a regular non-overnight social guest with a close familial relationship, weekly visits, and free rein of the premises had a reasonable expectation of privacy in the host's home and curtilage. Bringing contraband without permission did not eliminate that interest, although the court ultimately found no Fourth Amendment violation.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"standing/reasonable_expectation_of_privacy/social_guest/curtilage\",\"discovery_path\":\"Fourth Amendment standing and social-guest searches -> published Fourth Circuit opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/19-4348/19-4348-2024-07-02.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Chatrie (panel)\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-07-09\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"107 F.4th 319; No. 22-4489\",\"proposition\":\"Held that obtaining two hours of opted-in Google Location History through a geofence warrant was not a search because the defendant lacked a reasonable expectation of privacy in data exposed to Google; suppression was also rejected under good faith. The panel judgment was vacated upon rehearing en banc and later displaced by the Supreme Court.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"standing/REP/third_party_doctrine/geofence; good_faith/Leon\",\"discovery_path\":\"Geofence standing and good-faith searches -> published panel opinion and subsequent-opinion history: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/22-4489/22-4489-2024-07-09.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Davis\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eleventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-07-30\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-10184\",\"proposition\":\"Held that a defendant lacked Fourth Amendment standing to challenge geofence data obtained from another person's phone and Google account. The disclosed records contained no data from his device or account and merely allowed investigators to infer his movements indirectly; an alleged preliminary Google sweep of his account was insufficient absent disclosure to the government.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"standing/geofence/third_party_account/indirect_inference\",\"discovery_path\":\"Geofence and standing searches -> published Eleventh Circuit opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca11/23-10184/23-10184-2024-07-30.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Smith\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-08-09\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"110 F.4th 817; No. 23-60321\",\"proposition\":\"Held that the geofence warrant before it was a modern general warrant and categorically unconstitutional, creating a circuit conflict with the then-existing Chatrie panel approach. Nevertheless, Leon barred suppression because officers acted objectively reasonably amid novel and unsettled law; the court rejected a categorical rule that good faith can never save evidence obtained under a general warrant.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"good_faith/Leon/general_warrants/geofence/circuit_split\",\"discovery_path\":\"Geofence general-warrant and good-faith searches -> official published Fifth Circuit opinion: https://www.ca5.uscourts.gov/opinions/pub/23/23-60321-CR0.pdf\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Maher\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-10-30\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"120 F.4th 297; No. 23-6181\",\"proposition\":\"Held that police performed an unlawful search by opening a hash-matched file that Google had never viewed, but applied good faith because the Fifth and Sixth Circuits then authorized the practice and the contrary Ninth Circuit decision came later. In judging objective reliance, the Second Circuit considers sister-circuit authority and the broader decisional landscape, not solely binding precedent.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"good_faith/Davis/nonbinding_precedent/private_search/hash_match/circuit_split\",\"discovery_path\":\"Private-search hash-match and good-faith searches -> published Second Circuit opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca2/23-6181/23-6181-2024-10-30.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Chatrie (order granting rehearing en banc)\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit en banc\",\"date\":\"2024-11-01\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 22-4489\",\"proposition\":\"Granted rehearing en banc, thereby vacating the July 2024 panel judgment concerning geofence standing and good faith.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"en_banc_grant/vacatur/geofence/standing/good_faith\",\"discovery_path\":\"Panel subsequent-history review -> Fourth Circuit rehearing order: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/22-4489/22-4489-2024-11-01.html\",\"confidence\":1.0}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Holmes\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-11-13\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"121 F.4th 727; No. 22-10266\",\"proposition\":\"Held that a law-based good-faith claim requires binding appellate precedent specifically authorizing the challenged conduct; merely plausible or conflicting out-of-circuit law is insufficient, creating direct tension with Maher. It also rejected inevitable discovery because the government offered speculation about a discretionary parallel investigation rather than demonstrated historical facts and predictable routine procedures.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"good_faith/Davis/binding_precedent; inevitable_discovery/non_speculative_proof\",\"discovery_path\":\"Good-faith precedent and inevitable-discovery searches -> published Ninth Circuit opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca9/22-10266/22-10266-2024-11-13.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Magruder\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the District of Columbia Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-01-21\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"126 F.4th 671; No. 22-3025\",\"proposition\":\"Rejected the argument that an allegedly venue- or jurisdiction-defective Stored Communications Act GPS-ping warrant was void ab initio and categorically outside Leon. Suppression would have been unavailable because officers reasonably relied on the issuing magistrate, defeating the defendant's related ineffective-assistance claim.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"good_faith/Leon/Rule_41-SCA/jurisdictionally_defective_warrant\",\"discovery_path\":\"Void-ab-initio, SCA, and good-faith searches -> official D.C. Circuit opinion: https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USCOURTS-caDC-22-03025/pdf/USCOURTS-caDC-22-03025-0.pdf\",\"confidence\":0.98}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Chatrie (en banc)\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit en banc\",\"date\":\"2025-04-30\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"136 F.4th 100; No. 22-4489\",\"proposition\":\"Affirmed in a one-sentence en banc judgment. The court divided evenly over whether acquisition of Location History was a search, while the judges voting to affirm treated good faith as sufficient to deny suppression. The Supreme Court vacated this judgment in 2026.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"good_faith/Leon/geofence/en_banc_fracture/later_vacated\",\"discovery_path\":\"En banc geofence follow-up -> Fourth Circuit opinion history and Supreme Court syllabus: https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/609/25-112/\",\"confidence\":0.98}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Ray\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-06-03\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-4024\",\"proposition\":\"Held that good faith could not excuse NCIS agents' search of a phone when an unambiguous military warrant authorized seizure but not search. Objectively unreasonable execution beyond a warrant's clear scope is not saved by Leon; the majority aligned with the Tenth Circuit and limited contrary readings of First Circuit precedent.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"good_faith/Leon/warrant_execution/clear_scope_exceeded/circuit_position\",\"discovery_path\":\"Good-faith and warrant-scope searches -> published Fourth Circuit opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/24-4024/24-4024-2025-06-03.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Lowers\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-03-10\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-4546\",\"proposition\":\"Held that a Google Drive user retained a reasonable expectation of privacy despite boilerplate permitting automated scanning, and that a hash match did not frustrate privacy in an unopened file. Although the government's viewing was an unlawful search, later-discovered devices and statements were admissible under attenuation because of elapsed time, voluntary interviews and consent, intervening circumstances, and the absence of purposeful or flagrant misconduct; the court declined to rely on good faith.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"standing/REP/cloud_storage/private_search; fruits/attenuation/consent\",\"discovery_path\":\"Hash-match private-search, fruits, and attenuation searches -> published Fourth Circuit opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/24-4546/24-4546-2026-03-10.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Thorne\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the District of Columbia Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-03-17\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-3054\",\"proposition\":\"Held, in the D.C. Circuit's first direct treatment, that Leon applies to evidence obtained under a warrant allegedly issued beyond Federal Rule of Criminal Procedure 41(b)'s territorial authority. The court joined eleven sister circuits, rejected a void-ab-initio exception, and distinguished statutes such as Title III that expressly prescribe suppression.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"good_faith/Leon/Rule_41/jurisdictionally_defective_warrant/circuit_consensus\",\"discovery_path\":\"Rule 41 territorial-authority and good-faith searches -> published D.C. Circuit opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/cadc/23-3054/23-3054-2026-03-17.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"Chatrie v. United States\",\"court\":\"Supreme Court of the United States\",\"date\":\"2026-06-29\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"609 U.S. ___; No. 25-112\",\"proposition\":\"Held that government acquisition of an individual's Google Location History is a Fourth Amendment search because users retain a reasonable expectation of privacy in even short-duration location records held by Google; opt-in use does not trigger the ordinary third-party doctrine. The Court vacated the Fourth Circuit judgment and remanded for probable-cause and particularity review at each geofence step, while leaving the good-faith suppression question unresolved.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"standing/REP/location_history/third_party_doctrine; remedies/good_faith_reserved\",\"discovery_path\":\"Geofence standing and Supreme Court disposition searches -> Supreme Court opinion and syllabus: https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/609/25-112/\",\"confidence\":1.0}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Rosario\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-07-01\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-1242\",\"proposition\":\"Held that the government preserved good faith by arguing the substance of Leon's facial-deficiency inquiry even without consistently using the label. Leon shielded reliance on the Facebook warrant notwithstanding asserted particularity, overbreadth, and Franks defects because negligent drafting and isolated negligence did not establish deliberate, reckless, or grossly negligent police misconduct.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"good_faith/Leon/preservation-waiver/particularity/Franks/Herring_culpability\",\"discovery_path\":\"Good-faith waiver, Facebook-warrant, and Franks searches -> precedential Third Circuit opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca3/23-1242/23-1242-2026-07-01.html\",\"confidence\":0.98}"}}
